# Line endings here are MIXED, and that is the measured state, not the goal. At 2026-09-01:
# 23 of 72 blobs are LF-only (deadman/verify_certificate.py among them, entirely) and 4 test
# files are mixed inside themselves. It was already so when this file was added: 21 of 46 then.
# The sentence that used to sit here - "every blob in this repo is CRLF" - was false on the day
# it was written, and it was the premise the freeze was argued from.
#
# CORRECTION NOTE, 2026-09-03 - the census above is not rewritten, it is annotated, because it
# is dated and it is the record of what we believed. What it says about BLOBS is right. What it
# implies about AUTHORS is not, and that is the half every reader takes away from a line that
# opens "line endings here are MIXED, and that is the measured state".
#
# It counted stored blobs and concluded about what people wrote. Those are the same population
# only while nothing converts in between, and `core.autocrlf=true` was converting: it normalises
# CRLF to LF on the way IN, so a file written CRLF is stored LF and the working copy and the blob
# disagree in silence. `* -text` stops git converting from that point on; it does not undo what
# was already stored, and nothing surfaces the disagreement until the file is next committed.
#
# Recounted today, 84 tracked text files, with the LF blobs split by WHY they are LF:
#
#     blob CRLF                                  40
#     blob MIXED                                  4
#     blob LF                                    40
#         of those, working copy is CRLF         17   <- LF because git converted it
#         of those, working copy is LF           23   <- LF because somebody wrote it that way
#
# So the repository is less authored-mixed than the census reads: 17 of the 40 LF blobs are an
# artefact of a setting, not a choice anybody made. The specific claim about
# `deadman/verify_certificate.py` being LF entirely still holds - it is LF in the blob AND on
# disk, and it is not one of the 17.
#
# Two things follow, and neither is "normalise now".
#   - The three reasons below still hold. Nothing here argues for uniformity; the count moved,
#     the argument did not. Normalising remains closed.
#   - The 17 are a queue, not a defect: each will look like an ending change the first time it is
#     committed, because that is when the working bytes finally reach the blob.
#     scripts/check_line_endings.py names that shape as a diagnosis and deliberately WITHHOLDS
#     its ordinary remedy there - "rewrite it keeping its own endings" would rewrite every line
#     of a file nobody touched, which is the damage this whole mechanism exists to prevent.
#
# What `* -text` does, and it still holds: it stops GIT converting in either direction, so an
# edit that KEEPS a file's own endings does not rewrite it or move its blame. It promises
# nothing more - in particular it does NOT stop an EDITOR from writing a file back with other
# endings, which still lands as a whole-file diff (measured again 2026-09-01: 3,075 lines for a
# one-line change). Keep a file's own endings; check the diff size before committing.
#
# NORMALISING IS CLOSED - decided 2026-09-01, and NOT for the reason cc776d1 gave.
# That commit argued from "every blob is CRLF", false on the day it was written, and from
# "the whole-repo rewrite this prevents", also false: measured, converting everything to CRLF
# rewrites 27 of 67 text files and to LF rewrites 44 - never 67. The three reasons that hold:
#   1. Normalising does not touch what did the damage. The 3,075-line diff came from an EDITOR
#      rewriting endings, not from the repo being mixed; a uniform repo would not have stopped
#      it either. What protects is the tool matching the file, and we do not control every tool.
#   2. The blame cost is real, and it is 27 or 44 files - a true cost, not an inflated one.
#   3. Nothing depends on uniformity. `* -text` stops git converting and never promised more.
# What replaces it is a check on the property that matters instead of on a proxy for it:
# scripts/check_line_endings.py refuses a change that altered a file's endings, in either
# direction, mixed files included. Rule for an editor: keep the endings of the file you touch.
* -text
